course: biology
lesson: 373
title: Ukraine's steppe: marmots, sousliks, great bustards and Askania-Nova
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: On a grassy hill in eastern Ukraine a sharp whistle rings out, and a fat furry animal standing up like a post dives underground. It is a steppe marmot, bobak, warning its family that an eagle is overhead.
explain: The steppe is flat grassland with no trees to hide behind, so its animals live by watching far, standing up tall and running into burrows. Little sousliks do the same, and eagles and polecats hunt them.
explain: Once the steppe covered much of southern Ukraine. Almost all of it was ploughed into fields full of wheat and sunflowers, and its animals had to survive in reserves and on old burial mounds called kurgans.
explain: In the Kherson region, Askania-Nova, set up in 1898, kept a big piece of steppe never ploughed and helped save wild animals such as Przewalski's horses. Since 2022 it has been under Russian occupation, and Ukrainian scientists report worries about its animals.
fact: The steppe marmot, bobak, stands on its back legs and whistles to warn its family of eagles and foxes.
fact: Marmot families live in big burrows with many entrances and sleep through winter for about half the year.
fact: Before winter marmots get very fat; then their heart slows to a few beats a minute, the body cools, the family sleeps together for warmth and they plug the burrow with grass and earth.
fact: Marmots almost vanished when most steppe was ploughed and came back in reserves in the Luhansk and Kharkiv regions.
fact: Sousliks are ground squirrels of the steppe that eat seeds, roots and insects and stand up to watch for eagles, polecats and foxes; the speckled souslik has white dots and is now rare.
fact: Sousliks are a main food for steppe eagles, imperial eagles and steppe polecats, so when souslik colonies disappear these hunters disappear too.
fact: The great bustard is one of the heaviest birds that can fly; big males weigh about 16 kilograms, and it is in Ukraine's Red Book.
fact: A great bustard has three toes on each foot and no back toe, because it walks on open ground and never perches.
fact: In spring a male great bustard turns his feathers inside out to look like a white fluffy ball, puffs up his throat and fans his tail, and females watch many males before choosing.
fact: Askania-Nova in the Kherson region was set up in 1898 by Friedrich Falz-Fein as a nature reserve and zoo with animals from many continents, zebras and antelopes too, and it protects one of the biggest pieces of unploughed steppe.
fact: Przewalski's horses are wild horses from Mongolia that died out in the wild and survived in zoos; Askania-Nova helped breed them, and some were later let go in the Chornobyl zone.
fact: Przewalski's horses are short and stocky, sandy brown, with a stiff mane that stands up like a brush.
fact: Only a tiny part of real steppe is left, mostly in reserves and on old burial mounds called kurgans, where old steppe flowers and insects survive like on an island.
fact: Wild horses called tarpans lived on the Ukrainian steppe until the 1800s and saigas until a few hundred years ago; hunting and ploughing drove them away.
exercise: Why does a steppe marmot whistle? | To warn its family of eagles and foxes | To call birds to come and sing with it in the morning | To tell the farmer it is hungry | To scare away the rain
exercise: Why does a marmot family need many doors to its burrow? | So they can dive in fast from anywhere and escape if a hunter comes in | So more sunlight gets in | So they can keep cows inside | Because each door is for a different day of the week, and they take turns
exercise: How long do marmots sleep in winter? | About half the year | Only about one short week | Just one long winter night | They never sleep in winter
exercise: What happens to a marmot's heart during winter sleep? | It slows to a few beats a minute | It stops completely for months | It beats faster to keep warm | It grows twice as big
exercise: How do marmots keep the cold out of the burrow? | They plug it with grass and earth | They light a small fire to keep warm | They dig extra doors to let in fresh air | They sleep near the entrance in the snow
exercise: What do sousliks eat? | Seeds, roots and insects | Fish and frogs from ponds | Other sousliks | Only grass flowers that grow in the spring
exercise: Why do sousliks stand up straight by their holes? | To see eagles and foxes far across the flat grass | To get warm in the wind | To show off to the cows and horses grazing nearby | Because their legs are tired
exercise: Why can saving sousliks also save eagles? | Sousliks are a main food for steppe eagles | Eagles live inside souslik burrows | Sousliks build nests for eagles | Eagles eat the grass that sousliks plant
exercise: How heavy can a big male great bustard be? | About 16 kilograms | Only about 1 kilogram | About 160 kilograms | About 50 grams in all
exercise: Why has a great bustard no back toe? | It walks on open ground and never perches | It lost it in fights with other bustards | It swims and needs no toes | It hangs upside down from branches instead of perching
exercise: How does a male great bustard show off in spring? | He turns his feathers inside out to look like a white fluffy ball | He sings the loudest and longest song of all the birds on the steppe | He builds a nest of gold sticks | He dives into the river
exercise: When was Askania-Nova set up? | In 1898 | In 1998 | In 1066 | In 2022
exercise: Where did Przewalski's horses come from? | Mongolia | Australia | Southern Brazil | Northern Iceland
exercise: What does a Przewalski's horse's mane look like? | Short and stiff, standing up like a brush | Long and flowing down to its knees | Plaited into neat braids | It has no mane at all
exercise: Where did the old steppe go? | Almost all of it was ploughed for farms | It turned into a sandy desert by itself | It was flooded by the sea | It grew into thick forest of oak and pine
exercise: Where do old steppe flowers survive like on an island? | On old burial mounds called kurgans | In the middle of big wheat fields on farms | On the flat roofs of the village houses | At the muddy bottom of rivers and lakes
exercise: Which wild horse lived on the Ukrainian steppe until the 1800s? | The tarpan | The striped zebra | The mustang | The shire horse
test: Count them: a great bustard has 3 toes on each foot. How many toes on both feet? | 6 | 8 | 4 | 10
test: Count them: a marmot family of 7 stands up on their back legs. How many legs are touching the ground? | 14 | 28 | 7 | 21
test: Count them: 4 Przewalski's horses and 2 zebras graze at Askania-Nova. How many legs? | 24 | 16 | 12 | 20
test: In a cartoon a gopher pops up from its hole, chats all winter and never sleeps. What is right for a steppe marmot? | It sleeps through the winter for about half the year | It stays awake and eats all winter long under the snow | It flies south for the winter | It sleeps only on Sundays
test: «marmot's just lazy, sleeps half the year lol» Why is it really sleeping? | There is no food in winter, so it saves energy by sleeping | Marmots are sick in winter | It is too fat to walk, so it stays in bed until the fat melts | Marmots hate snow and sulk
test: Why does a fat marmot sleep through winter better than a thin one? | It has more stored fat to live on until spring | Fat marmots sleep less | Thin marmots wake up warmer and stronger in the spring | Fat marmots eat in their sleep
test: «bustard's just a big chicken, can't fly» What is right? | It can fly, and it is one of the heaviest birds that can | It cannot fly at all, it only runs very fast like an ostrich | It only flies at night | It is a kind of turkey
test: What would happen to the steppe eagles if all souslik colonies disappeared? | They would lose a main food and disappear too | They would be happier with more space | They would start eating grass | Nothing would change for them
test: «zebras in Ukraine?? that's fake» What is right? | Askania-Nova keeps animals from many continents, zebras too | Zebras have always lived wild in Ukraine | The photo must be from Africa, because zebras cannot live in cold places | Zebras are a kind of Ukrainian horse
test: Why might a zoo be the last home of a wild animal like Przewalski's horse? | It died out in the wild but survived and was bred in zoos | Zoos are where all wild animals first come from | Wild animals prefer cages | Zoos made it by mixing different farm horses together
test: What would a steppe need before a big grazer like the saiga could return? | Big protected grassland with no hunting or ploughing | More wheat fields and roads | Tall forests to hide in | Lots of zoos close by, so sick saigas can be taken in quickly
exam: Identify the principal function of the whistle emitted by the steppe marmot. | To warn members of its family of approaching predators | To attract a mate in autumn | To signal the onset of rain | To summon birds to its burrow
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the feet of the great bustard. | Each foot bears three toes and no hind toe, consistent with life on open ground | Each foot bears four toes adapted to perching | The feet are webbed for swimming | Each foot bears a single toe
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the present extent of the Ukrainian steppe? | Only a minute fraction survives, principally in reserves and upon ancient burial mounds | It continues to cover the greater part of southern Ukraine | It has been entirely converted into forest | It has expanded considerably during the past century
exam: Ascertain the manner in which the Przewalski's horse was preserved after it had perished in the wild. | It was bred in zoos | It was cloned from bones | It was found again in Brazil | It was crossed from zebras
